"""Year summaries, 1976-2000. See _year_summaries_1926_1950.py for notes on sourcing and accuracy."""
S = {
1976: "The recovery from the 1974-75 recession continued, with growth around 5 percent and inflation easing to roughly 5 to 6 percent. The Dow gained about 18 percent, ending near 1,004 after touching its highest levels since 1973. Jimmy Carter, a Georgia governor and outsider, won the presidency in November. Steve Jobs and Steve Wozniak founded Apple Computer on 1 April, a milestone in the personal computer era, though few investors noticed. The Fed under Arthur Burns kept policy relatively accommodative, and the dollar was stable. Britain turned to the IMF for a loan as sterling slid. Beneath the calm, the structural causes of stagflation, including productivity weakness and energy dependence, remained unresolved.",
1977: "Stagflation persisted, with inflation near 6.5 percent and unemployment above 7 percent, and the stock market fell about 17 percent in the Dow as investors lost faith in policy. The dollar weakened as the trade deficit widened, partly from oil imports, and gold started to climb. The Trans-Alaska Pipeline began pumping oil, and the New York blackout in July exposed urban fragility. The personal computer era began in earnest with the Apple II, Commodore PET and TRS-80 all reaching consumers. President Carter proposed an energy plan, calling it the 'moral equivalent of war'. The Fed was led by Burns until early 1978, and rising inflation expectations were beginning to feed into wage demands.",
1978: "Inflation climbed past 7 percent and the dollar fell sharply against the yen and Deutsche mark, prompting the Fed and Treasury to take emergency steps in November, including a rise in the discount rate to a record 9.5 percent and a large currency support package. G. William Miller became Fed chair in March, but credibility was weak. The Dow gained little over the year with a strong rebound late in the year after a sharp autumn fall. The Camp David Accords were signed in September, and the Revenue Act cut capital gains taxes, supporting equities. The Humphrey-Hawkins Act formalised the Fed's dual mandate for employment and price stability. Gold rose past $200 an ounce.",
1979: "A year of regime change. Paul Volcker became Fed chair in August, and on 6 October the Fed announced it would target money-supply growth rather than interest rates, allowing rates to rise sharply to break inflation. The Iranian revolution in January and the subsequent oil supply disruption pushed crude prices above $30 a barrel, and inflation reached about 11 percent. The Three Mile Island accident in March damaged nuclear power's prospects, and Chrysler sought a federal bailout. Gold soared toward $500 as investors lost confidence in paper money, and the dollar stabilised after the Fed's new approach. Soviet troops entered Afghanistan in December. The Volcker shift is one of the most important monetary events of the century.",
1980: "Interest rates reached extremes: the federal funds rate approached 20 percent, the prime rate topped 20 percent, and a short recession in the first half was followed by a brief recovery. Silver Thursday on 27 March saw the Hunt brothers' attempt to corner the silver market collapse, rattling brokerage firms and forcing a rescue loan. Gold hit about $850 an ounce in January before falling. Inflation was around 13 percent, and Ronald Reagan won the presidency in November on a promise of tax cuts and a strong dollar. Apple went public in December in the most successful IPO to that point. The Dow ended the year up about 15 percent despite high rates, boosted by a late rally.",
1981: "Rates peaked in a hard-money campaign: the prime rate reached about 21.5 percent in January, mortgage rates exceeded 18 percent, and the economy entered a severe recession in July that would last until late 1982. The Reagan administration passed large tax cuts and a defence build-up under the Economic Recovery Tax Act, opening large deficits, while the air traffic controllers' strike in August was broken. The dollar surged and long-term Treasury yields reached about 15 percent, among the highest on record, which set up the bond bull market of the next four decades. The stock market fell about 10 percent in the year as high rates weighed on valuations. IBM launched its personal computer in August.",
1982: "A severe recession, with unemployment reaching 10.8 percent in November, ended the inflationary spiral: inflation fell below 4 percent. In August the Mexican government announced it could not service its debt, igniting the Latin American debt crisis that threatened major US banks, and the Fed cut rates and shifted policy as Volcker abandoned strict monetary targeting. The Dow bottomed at about 777 on 12 August and then surged, starting a secular bull market that would run until 2000, with the index gaining about 20 percent in 1982. Falling interest rates and cheaper oil helped. Penn Square Bank failed in July, and the Garn-St Germain Act deregulated savings and loan institutions, sowing seeds of a later crisis.",
1983: "A strong economic recovery with growth above 6 percent as the earlier tight money gave way to lower rates and the Reagan tax cuts took hold. Unemployment fell from its peak and inflation stayed near 3 percent. Technology stocks boomed, particularly personal computers and software, and investors flocked to initial public offerings, though a shakeout in home computers would follow in 1984. The Dow gained about 20 percent. The agreement to break up AT&T into regional operating companies, reached in January 1982 and approved by the court, was set to take effect in 1984. Budget deficits rose, and the dollar strengthened sharply, hurting exporters. The Fed under Volcker kept policy cautious.",
1984: "The AT&T break-up took effect on 1 January, creating seven regional companies and a competitive long-distance market, a landmark in deregulation. Continental Illinois, the seventh-largest US bank, was rescued by the FDIC in May in the biggest bank bailout to date, establishing the 'too big to fail' idea. The Apple Macintosh launched in January with the famous Super Bowl commercial. The Dow was nearly flat, down slightly, as the Fed tightened mid-year and the dollar kept rising, hurting manufacturers. Growth slowed after a very strong first half. Reagan won a landslide reelection in November. Interest rates peaked and began to decline, and the federal budget deficit became a major concern.",
1985: "The Plaza Accord on 22 September saw the US, Japan, Germany, France and Britain agree to coordinate intervention to bring the overvalued dollar down, a deliberate reversal after the dollar's rise. The dollar fell sharply over the next two years, helping US exporters but sending shockwaves through Japan, which would respond with easy monetary policy that fuelled its bubble. Stocks gained about 27 percent as the Dow crossed 1,500 and the Fed eased. Mikhail Gorbachev took power in the Soviet Union in March. Oil prices began to weaken, and farm and savings-and-loan stress grew. The Gramm-Rudman Act attempted to constrain deficits.",
1986: "Oil prices collapsed from about $27 to under $10 a barrel as Saudi Arabia increased output, hurting energy producers and Texas banks while helping consumers and holding down inflation. The Tax Reform Act, signed in October, cut rates and eliminated many loopholes, including real estate shelters that contributed to the later property bust. In London the 'Big Bang' on 27 October deregulated the stock exchange and opened it to electronic trading and foreign firms. The Dow gained about 22 percent, rising above 1,900. The Challenger shuttle disaster in January and Iran-Contra revelations in November hit confidence. Insider-trading scandals, including Ivan Boesky, broke open.",
1987: "Black Monday, 19 October, saw the Dow fall 22.6 percent in a single day, its largest one-day percentage drop ever, after a run-up of about 40 percent earlier in the year to a record near 2,700. Portfolio insurance strategies, program trading, a weakening dollar, rising rates and trade tensions were blamed, and the NYSE was overwhelmed by volume. The Fed under new chair Alan Greenspan quickly announced it would provide liquidity, helping prevent a wider crisis, and the market stabilised. The economy was not much affected, and the Dow finished the year slightly higher. The crash led to circuit breakers and a review of market structure and made Greenspan's reputation for crisis management.",
1988: "Markets recovered as the economy avoided a recession, and the Dow gained about 12 percent. The Fed raised rates through the year to stay ahead of inflation, which edged up to about 4 percent. Japan's asset bubble was inflating, with the Nikkei climbing and land prices soaring, and Japanese firms bought US real estate and trophy assets. In the US the savings-and-loan crisis deepened as hundreds of institutions failed, and the Basel Accord set the first international bank capital standards in July. George H. W. Bush won the presidential election in November. Drexel Burnham Lambert pleaded guilty to securities fraud charges in the Milken investigation, and leveraged buyouts, including RJR Nabisco, reached record size.",
1989: "The Berlin Wall fell on 9 November, ending the Cold War division of Europe and opening Eastern Europe to markets. The Nikkei peaked on 29 December at nearly 38,900, the top of Japan's bubble, after which stocks and land prices began a decade-long decline. In the US a mini-crash on 13 October, triggered by the collapse of a leveraged buyout of United Airlines, took the Dow down 6.9 percent in a day, but the market recovered and finished the year up about 27 percent. The Financial Institutions Reform, Recovery and Enforcement Act created the Resolution Trust Corporation to resolve failed S&Ls. The Exxon Valdez oil spill occurred in March, and the junk bond market weakened.",
1990: "Japan's bubble burst in earnest as the Nikkei lost nearly half its value over the year. Iraq invaded Kuwait on 2 August, sending oil above $40 a barrel and pushing the US into a recession that began in July, with the Dow falling about 20 percent from its summer high to an October low before recovering. Drexel Burnham Lambert filed for bankruptcy in February, ending the junk bond era's leading bank. The Fed cut rates as the economy weakened and credit tightened, with the savings-and-loan crisis weighing on lending. German reunification took place in October. Bush's tax increase deal in the autumn broke his campaign pledge, and commercial real estate prices began to fall.",
1991: "The Gulf War began in January and the quick allied victory triggered a strong rally. The US recession ended in March, though the recovery was sluggish and unemployment kept rising until mid-1992, a 'jobless recovery'. The Fed cut rates repeatedly, bringing the funds rate down to 4 percent by year end. The Soviet Union dissolved in December following a failed coup in August, ending the Cold War. In August the World Wide Web became publicly available, though its significance was not yet understood, and Linux was announced. The Dow gained about 20 percent, while the collapse of the Bank of Credit and Commerce International and the Salomon Brothers Treasury auction scandal marked financial misconduct. Japan's slump deepened.",
1992: "Black Wednesday on 16 September saw the UK forced out of the European Exchange Rate Mechanism after the pound came under heavy speculative attack, with George Soros famously profiting by about $1 billion. Sterling fell sharply, and Italy also left the ERM, signalling the fragility of fixed-rate regimes. In the US the economy slowly recovered, with the Fed holding rates near 3 percent, and Bill Clinton defeated George H. W. Bush in November on a platform that focused on the economy. The Dow gained about 4 percent. The Los Angeles riots in April followed the Rodney King verdict. The Maastricht Treaty ratification was contested and currency markets were volatile.",
1993: "The early internet era began as the Mosaic web browser was released, making the web accessible to ordinary users, though stocks barely reacted. The Fed held the funds rate at 3 percent, and long-term yields fell as bond markets rallied on Clinton's deficit-reduction package and low inflation. The Dow gained about 14 percent. NAFTA was ratified in November, and the European Union was formally established. The long period of low rates encouraged investors to chase yield through leveraged bond positions and derivatives, setting up the bond market rout of the next year. Several emerging markets attracted heavy inflows, and a stock market boom in emerging economies was developing.",
1994: "The 'great bond massacre'. The Fed surprised markets by raising the funds rate in February, and kept going, doubling it from 3 percent to 6 percent by February 1995, and bond prices fell in one of the worst bond markets on record, with 10-year yields rising from about 5.2 to over 8 percent. Leveraged investors and derivatives users suffered losses, including Orange County, California, which filed for bankruptcy in December after losing about $1.6 billion, the largest municipal failure at the time. The Mexican peso crisis erupted in December after a devaluation, raising fears of emerging-market contagion. The Dow finished roughly flat for the year.",
1995: "Netscape went public on 9 August, with shares more than doubling on the first day, an event often seen as the starting gun of the internet boom. Microsoft launched Windows 95, bringing graphical computing to the mass market. The Fed engineered a soft landing, cutting rates in July and December after the previous year's tightening, and the Dow gained about 33 percent, crossing 5,000 in November. The US assembled a rescue package for Mexico of roughly $50 billion, calming emerging markets. Japan's Kobe earthquake in January and Barings Bank's collapse from rogue trader Nick Leeson's losses in February were notable shocks. Productivity gains started to show.",
1996: "The boom gathered pace as technology stocks surged, Yahoo and several other internet companies went public, and the Dow crossed 6,000 in October. On 5 December Fed chairman Alan Greenspan asked whether 'irrational exuberance' had inflated asset values, briefly shaking global markets, but the rally resumed. The economy grew about 3.7 percent with unemployment near 5 percent and low inflation, a combination that later became known as the 'Goldilocks' economy. Clinton won reelection. The Telecommunications Act, signed in February, deregulated phone and cable markets, and the Dow gained about 26 percent. Asian economies were showing signs of strain from overinvestment and pegged currencies.",
1997: "The Asian financial crisis began on 2 July when Thailand floated the baht, and contagion spread to Indonesia, South Korea, Malaysia and the Philippines, with sharp currency collapses and IMF rescues. A mini-crash on 27 October, which saw the Dow fall 7.2 percent and trading halts triggered, followed Hong Kong's stock plunge, but US markets recovered quickly. Amazon went public in May, and the S&P 500 gained about 31 percent on strong earnings and low rates. Hedge fund manager Soros and Malaysian leader Mahathir clashed over currency speculation. The Fed held rates steady at 5.5 percent, and the dollar rose strongly. The crisis hinted at risks that would culminate the following year.",
1998: "Russia defaulted on its domestic debt and devalued the rouble in August, triggering a global flight to quality, and in September the hedge fund Long-Term Capital Management, highly leveraged and caught on the wrong side of convergence trades, required a $3.6 billion private-sector rescue organised by the New York Fed. The Fed cut rates three times in autumn to restore liquidity, and the stock market, after falling about 19 percent in the summer, rebounded to end the year with the S&P 500 up about 27 percent. Google was founded in September and internet stocks soared. The euro's launch was planned for January 1999, and Brazil faced its own currency crisis.",
1999: "The final phase of the dot-com bubble. The Nasdaq Composite rose about 85.6 percent, the best year in its history, led by internet, telecom and software companies often trading on revenue or eyeballs rather than profit, and the Dow crossed 10,000 in March. The Fed raised rates three times from June as the economy overheated. Congress repealed Glass-Steagall in November with the Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act, allowing banks, insurers and brokers to combine. Fears of the Y2K computer bug led to heavy spending and extra liquidity. Initial public offerings soared, with first-day gains frequently above 100 percent. The euro launched on 1 January, and the combined effect set up the 2000 peak.",
2000: "The Nasdaq peaked on 10 March at 5,048 and ended the year down about 39 percent as the dot-com bubble burst, with many internet stocks losing 80 to 90 percent. The Fed raised rates to 6.5 percent by May, its last hike, and then paused as the economy slowed. AOL's acquisition of Time Warner, announced in January, became a symbol of the excess. The contested presidential election between George W. Bush and Al Gore was resolved by the Supreme Court in December, and the economy tipped toward recession by year end. Telecom companies had heavily overinvested in fibre capacity and began to fail. The S&P 500 fell about 10 percent.",
}
